text:
Hello HN!<p>Wonderhop is a small browser game with no account needed to play, everyday there are 4 rounds of 7 quesitons like &quot;Name a food that a lot of people hate&quot;
The goal depends on the mode: Either gives the answer most people would give, the one no one would give, the oldest answer or the most recent one and there&#x27;s a leaderboard for the day.<p>The most interesting part of the project was understanding answers that are typed freely and in 10 languages, there was some typos, plurals, nickname, or even duplicate way of sayign the same thing.
We needed AI judge that checks it really existes conencted to some API like wikipedia to judge the veracity of each answer really quickly (less than 1s on average)<p>The percentage starts from an estimate but move really fast with the answers of real players. If the list missed a good answer, it gets added as soon as a few different players give it.<p>(There are also a lot of secrets and pop culture references added in the game :) )<p>A large part of the code was written with AI tools, but the artistic direction the concept and every choice design are human-made<p>We&#x27;d love to know if the goal is clear from the very first question while playing and were any good answers rejected unfairly<p>We are a couple of developers from Montreal that like guessing games so we built this in about two month.<p>Thanks a lot!

&gt; Unfortunately, this perception of Gentoo remains, and I wish they would remove the verbiage from their site.<p>Based on what I can see after putting a minute or two into searching, that verbiage being referred to only appears on the About page. [0]<p>The distro&#x27;s home page says<p><pre><code>  Welcome to Gentoo, a highly flexible, source-based Linux distribution. 
</code></pre>
and has used those words since at least January 2023 [1]. Its home page has spoken about <i>flexibility</i> and has not spoken about <i>performance</i> since the big redesign in April <i>2016</i>... seven years prior to the publication of that guy&#x27;s blog post. [2][3]<p>And -speaking as a Gentoo user since 2002- neither the user community nor the maintainer community hold the opinion that Ultimate Maxxximum Performance is the distro&#x27;s goal. Trouble reports that come from &quot;ricers&quot; pretty much always get told &quot;Set your compiler flags to something sane (try &#x27;-O2 -pipe&#x27; or &#x27;-O2 -pipe -march=native&#x27;) and rebuild. If you can still repro the problem, file a new report because this one is getting closed INVALID.&quot;.<p>And -while the old Gentoo Wiki burned down so I can&#x27;t <i>prove</i> it- [4] the advice about GCC optimization in the wiki has always cautioned against using aggressive compiler flags and warned that doing so will -among other things- <i>not</i> substantially improve performance. The advice about reasonable compiler flags has pretty consistently been &quot;Use -O2, -pipe, -march=$THE_CORRECT_SETTING_FOR_YOUR_CPU, and <i>maybe</i> -fomit-frame-pointer&quot;... though the &quot;-fomit-frame-pointer&quot; advice has <i>always</i> come with a warning that doing that may make debugging either extremely difficult or impossible, and the &quot;-march&quot; advice the warning that this may make it impossible to run those binaries on another computer.<p>Anyway. There&#x27;s only <i>so</i> much you can do to erase an -allegedly- pervasive myth. The fact that some folks have the belief that Gentoo Linux is all about Maxxximum Performance doesn&#x27;t change the fact that nearly all of the people who use it and -almost certainly- all of the people who maintain it <i>strongly</i> disagree with that claim. The distro is and always has been about flexibility, user choice, and having fun. And it&#x27;s a nice bonus if building for -say- -march=athlon-xp instead of -march=i586 <i>happens</i> to get you a bit of extra performance here and there.<p>[0] &lt;<a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.gentoo.org&#x2F;get-started&#x2F;about&#x2F;" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.gentoo.org&#x2F;get-started&#x2F;about&#x2F;</a>&gt;<p>[1] &lt;<a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;web.archive.org&#x2F;web&#x2F;20230101024843&#x2F;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.gentoo.org&#x2F;" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;web.archive.org&#x2F;web&#x2F;20230101024843&#x2F;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.gento...</a>&gt;<p>[2] &lt;<a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;web.archive.org&#x2F;web&#x2F;20150406115846&#x2F;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.gentoo.org&#x2F;" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;web.archive.org&#x2F;web&#x2F;20150406115846&#x2F;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.gento...</a>&gt;<p>[3] That year&#x27;s April 1 redesign was a bit cool, IMO.<p>[4] The oldest copy that I can easily find is from mid 2013 [5], so you&#x27;ll just have to trust me that this has been the long-standing opinion of the overwhelming majority of users and maintainers. While you&#x27;re there, take a minute or two to skim the prose and notice how it <i>consistently</i> warns against using aggressive compiler flags.<p>[5] &lt;<a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;web.archive.org&#x2F;web&#x2F;20130829083155&#x2F;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;wiki.gentoo.org&#x2F;wiki&#x2F;GCC_optimization" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;web.archive.org&#x2F;web&#x2F;20130829083155&#x2F;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;wiki.gent...</a>&gt;
